**user**:
> Can I use an exponential function to approximate annual discounting?
**assistant**:
> Yes, you can use an exponential function to approximate annual discounting, and it's actually a common practice. In finance and economics, the process of discounting is often represented with an exponential function because it reflects the compound effect over time.
> 
> The exponential discounting formula is typically expressed as:
> 
> \[ PV = \frac{FV}{(1 + r)^n} \]
> 
> However, when using continuous compounding, it is often expressed as:
> 
> \[ PV = FV \times e^{-rt} \]
> 
> Where:
> - \( PV \) is the present value,
> - \( FV \) is the future value,
> - \( r \) is the annual discount rate (expressed as a decimal),
> - \( n \) is the number of years into the future the payment occurs,
> - \( e \) is the base of the natural logarithm,
> - \( t \) is the time in years.
> 
> The exponential discounting model assumes a constant discount rate over time and is conceptually linked to the idea of continuous compounding. It is widely used because it simplifies the complexities of compounding discounts over time, though one should be cautious about its assumptions, such as the constancy of interest rates over long periods.
